# Workflow: coding tasks

## 0. Choose the route

- **Do it yourself, no team:** the change is confined to one file, changes no
  behavior and is a handful of lines: a string, a label, a comment, a constant.
- **Team:** everything else, anything touching logic, data, configuration or
  more than one file. When in doubt, take the team route.
- **Several independent tickets:** split them and run one coder per ticket in
  parallel, each with `isolation: "worktree"` so they cannot collide. Never let
  two agents work against the same database or service at the same time.

## 1. Team

- `TeamCreate` with team name `coding`, then `TaskCreate` for the work.
- Spawn the `coder` with the full task: files, requirements, constraints. First
  instruction in its prompt: `Run: printf '\033]2;Coder: <short summary>\033\\'`
  to set its pane title.
- Once you took the team route, do not write the code yourself.

## 2. tracer, available to everyone

`tracer` is read-only and maps dependency and execution chains. You may call it
directly to diagnose before choosing an approach; `coder` and `reviewer` call it
themselves. Use it whenever a diagnosis is uncertain, a first fix failed, or the
change touches derived values, extended code or timing-sensitive code. It
returns a report, never a patch.

## 3. Review

When the coder is done, spawn the `reviewer` with the changed files and the
original task (pane title `Reviewer: <short summary>`).

- Findings go back to the coder via `SendMessage`, the fixes back to the
  reviewer. At most three rounds; after that, report to the user.

## 4. Verification

When the review passes, spawn the `verifier` (pane title
`Verifier: <short summary>`).

- Gate 1 (scoped linters, type checks, the covering tests) always runs.
- Gate 2 (full suite, restarts, migrations, anything disruptive) runs only after
  you asked the user and wrote the authorization into the verifier's prompt.
- A gate 1 failure goes back to the coder. A gate 2 failure caused by the
  environment, not the code, is a finding for the user.

## 5. Behavior in the browser

`ui-inspector` drives the running application and reports what the screen does.
Use it when the open question is behavior, not building. A deviation it finds
goes to the coder.

## 6. Finish

- Report what was done, that the review passed and which gates actually ran.
  Name what remains unverified.
- **Never commit before the user has tested.** Then offer the commit. Subagents
  never commit, push or stage.
- Shut the teammates down and delete the team.

## 7. Close the loop

A review finding the coder should have known is a gap in the setup. Append one
line to `~/.claude/vault/knowledge/review-recurring-findings.md`: the rule, the
date and the project. When a rule has been hit three times, propose to the user
to promote it into the coder's definition or into a hook.

# Rules

- The user talks only to you, the main agent. Keep them informed with short
  status updates.
- If a task is unclear, ask before creating the team.
- Questions, explanations and configuration changes are handled by you directly,
  without a team.
